.TH FKREPLACE 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkreplace, fkreplacec \- replace substrings of an fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkreplace(fkstring *" fks ", const fkstring *" old ,
.BI "                    const fkstring *" new ", size_t " max_count );
.BI "fkstring *fkreplacec(fkstring *" fks ", const char *" old ,
.BI "                    const char *" new ", size_t " max_count );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkreplace ()
replaces occurrences of
.I old
in
.I fks
with
.IR new ,
in place.
.BR fkreplacec ()
does the same with NUL-terminated
.I old
and
.IR new ;
.I fks
is still searched in full, past any embedded NUL bytes.
.PP
.I fks
is scanned from left to right.
Matches do not overlap (\(dqaaa\(dq contains one \(dqaa\(dq), and
replacement text is never scanned again.
At most
.I max_count
matches are replaced, or all of them if
.I max_count
is 0.
An empty
.I old
matches nothing, so the call changes nothing.
.PP
The matches are counted before anything is changed, so
.I fks
is reallocated at most once however many there are,
and not at all when there are none.
.I old
and
.I new
may be
.I fks
itself, or point into its buffer.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.IR fks ,
or NULL, leaving
.I fks
unchanged, if any argument is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not return on allocation failure, nor when the
resulting length overflows
.BR size_t ;
see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s = fkstrnew("a\-b\-c");
\&
fkreplacec(s, "\-", ", ", 0);   /* "a, b, c" */
fkreplacec(s, ", ", "", 1);    /* "ab, c" */
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrfind (3),
.BR fkinsert (3),
.BR fkremove (3)
